# Names and where they live

An HQL identifier is a name for a type, a step, a binding, a parameter or a
field. Each name has a **provider** — the core, an extension, the run context,
or the program — and a **namespace** that resolves it. The spelling of a name
follows one convention whichever provider supplies it, so a reader cannot tell a
core step from an extension step from a program binding by its case; the
provider is recorded where the name is bound, not in its spelling. The decision
record is [HQL-0011](../../proposals/HQL-0011/README.md).

## Lexical form

An identifier begins with a letter or `_` and continues with letters, digits
and `_`; letters are Unicode alphabetic. Names are case-sensitive: `Card` and
`card` are different names. The grammar does not classify a name by its case —
no rule reads an uppercase initial as a type or a constructor — so casing is a
convention and never a syntax error. A checker warning for a departure is
design direction, specified by HQL-0011. `true` and `false`
are the only words the lexer reserves; `type`, `abstract`, `import`, `private`
and the other keywords are recognized by position. `internal`, before a
declaration, is reserved and refused
([HQL-0028](../../proposals/HQL-0028/README.md)).

## Casing

| Kind of name | Convention | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Type, concrete or abstract | `PascalCase` | `Card`, `HmdCard`, `KnowledgeGraph`, `Seq` |
| Type parameter | one uppercase letter, or `PascalCase` | `T`, `K`, `V`, `S`, `N`, `E` |
| Step, operation, function | `snake_case` | `size`, `unwrap_or`, `and_then`, `downlinks` |
| Binding, parameter, named argument | `snake_case` | `bird_cards`, `first_two`, `by`, `default` |
| Declared field | `snake_case` | `metadata`, `provenance`, `approximate` |
| Extension | one lowercase word | `graph`, `format`, `lexical`, `semantic` |
| Keyword | lowercase | `type`, `abstract`, `import`, `match`, `given` |

An acronym inside a type name is written as a word: `HmdCard`, `IndexRef`,
`ModelRef`. A predicate step is named `is_<property>` — `is_some`, `is_none`.
A function is named for the value it returns, without a `get_` prefix:
`size(xs)`, `downlinks(card)`. The core step `get(map, key)` is lookup itself,
not a prefix. Where this card is silent, HQL follows the Rust naming
conventions of RFC 430.

The checker lints each name where a program introduces it — a binding, a
parameter, a `fn`, a `type`, a type parameter and a declared field. A name off
its kind's convention is a warning at the name, naming the kind and the
convention, and never changes the exit status. Every step the binary registers
and every name in `std/` follows the convention, which a test enforces
([HQL-0011](../../proposals/HQL-0011/README.md)).

Three kinds of name are not HQL identifiers. The first two follow no HQL
convention:

- **Document names.** `@[[alice]]` names a document in the vault namespace by
  its file stem, which the author chose. A doc reference is not an identifier
  and is never linted.
- **`Data` keys.** The keys of a [[data-type|Data]] tree belong to whoever wrote
  the header, so `header.created_at` and `header.createdAt` are both correct
  readings of what is there. A data literal may quote a key that is not an
  identifier: `{ "created-at": d }`. Only a field a type *declares* is an HQL
  name — see [[fields]].
- **Source ids and kinds.** A data source is named in the run context's
  configuration, and a program reaches it with a string, `source("field_notes")`.
  Ids and kind names (`vault`, `git_vault`, `postgres`) are `snake_case`, so a
  binding can carry the id's name: `field_notes = source("field_notes")`. Data
  sources are design direction, specified by
  [HQL-0012](../../proposals/HQL-0012/README.md).

The value constructors of the core's sum types are spelled two ways today:
`some`, `none`, `ok` and `err` in lowercase, `Less`, `Equal` and `Greater` in
`PascalCase`. One convention for constructors is not yet stated. A record's
constructor is its type's name, `Point(x = 1, y = 2)`.

Three names are reserved by position, not by the lexer: `self`, the first
parameter of a receiver function or a trait's function, which `value.f()`
supplies; `Self`, the implementing type inside a `trait` or an `impl`; and a
module's local name, the last segment of `import a.b` or its `as` alias, which
qualifies the module's functions as `b.f(…)` and may not be a binding at the
same time ([HQL-0024](../../proposals/HQL-0024/README.md)).

## Providers

**The core** provides the type lattice and the collection steps. The lattice is
declared in [`std/`](../../../std/README.md), one file per module, and the core
steps — `size`, `contains`, `get`, `count`, `sort`, `take`, `filter`, `map`,
`of_type`, `is`, `group`, `reduce`, `sum`, `min`, `max`, `debug` — are always in
scope. See [[hql/std-lib]] and [[hql/core]].

**An extension** provides steps, registered in the binary and bound by
`import`. Today an extension names types the lattice already has and adds no
case to it. Design direction in HQL-0012 widens this by one thing: an extension
provides source kinds and their concrete types, such as `Vault` under the core's
abstract `VaultSource`. See [[hql/extensions]].

**The run context** — the configuration and host the binary runs in — provides
context values before a program starts, into the **program module** alone:
`vault`, `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr` and `file`, and the program module's
bindings over `vault`, `cards` and `graph`; by HQL-0012 every source named in
configuration. No module sees them
([HQL-0028](../../proposals/HQL-0028/README.md)).

**The program** provides bindings: `bird_cards = cards | filter(…)` introduces
`bird_cards` for the statements after it. A lambda's parameter — `c` in
`c => c.metadata.genre` — is a binding scoped to that lambda.

Types are provided by the core or by an extension, never by a program. A
program-defined function that acts as a pipeline step — an operation by
[HQL-0005](../../proposals/HQL-0005/README.md) — is design direction: `fn`
declarations are not in the grammar.

## Namespaces

| Namespace | Holds | Resolved by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Types | every type name, one lattice | the `std/` declarations and the binary's registry |
| Steps and functions | step and function names, per module | the prelude and `import`; `module.f(…)` qualifies one, and the receiver's type tells two modules declaring one name apart |
| Modules | the local names of imported modules | `import a.b [as c]`, the prelude's modules, and the standard library's canonical names |
| Traits | trait names, with their functions | the core's operator traits, the program's `trait` declarations, and the traits of imported modules |
| Bindings | program bindings and host-seeded context values | lexical scope: a binding is visible after the statement that makes it |
| Fields | declared fields, per type | the type of the value the `.` path is read from |
| Documents | file stems in the vault | the HyperMarkDown resolver — see [[hypermarkdown]] |
| Sources | ids in the run context's configuration | `source(id)`, at check time and offline (design direction) |

Two visible modules may declare one function name, `contains` of a String and
of a collection: the receiver's type tells them apart at the call, a call that
fits both is refused naming both, and the qualified form `module.f(…)` reaches
one of them. Two imports may not share a local name; `as` renames one. The host seeds context values into the binding namespace of the
program module before a program runs: `vault`, of type `Vault`, and
`knowledge`. `cards` is the program module's binding `cards = vault.cards` —
see [[card-type]].

## Import units and visibility

Every module, and the program module of an invocation, is one **import
unit**: its own imports and the prelude are visible in it and nowhere else. A
module is a closure: a free name in it resolves through its own declarations
and its own imports, never through the program module or a host binding.

A unit's own declaration wins over an imported one of the same name, in every
spelling — `s.f()`, `s | f` and `f(s)` — and a use written above that
declaration is refused rather than reaching the import's. Two imported
declarations of one name, neither the unit's own, are an ambiguity whatever
the import order, the prelude's modules included; qualifying the call,
`string.trim(s)`, resolves it.

Inside a module a declaration is public unless it is marked `private`. A
private function, receiver function, type, trait or binding is visible within
its own module only: no importer reaches it by name, as a receiver call or as
a step, and a public signature may not mention a private type or trait.
`private` is refused on an `impl` and in the program module, whose
declarations are all public. See [HQL-0028](../../proposals/HQL-0028/README.md).

See [[type-system]] for how a type name relates to another, and [[pipes]] for
what a step is.
